 \section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}
 
-The practical reading is a decision rule\rev{, and it is a rule for the two tasks evaluated
-here: SR-versus-AF rhythm separation on PTB-XL and N-vs-V beat morphology on MIT-BIH}. If a
-strong public foundation model can be run
-on balanced data in the target 12-lead format, ECGFounder gives the best unsupervised
-separation here. If the data are imbalanced or siloed across sites, if interpretability or
-low dimensionality matters, or if the leads on hand are not the standard twelve, the 16
-hand-crafted features are the better choice: they are within reach of ECGFounder on
-balanced data\rev{, overtake it under class imbalance, and hold far more of their clustering
-ARI under site heterogeneity, both measured on the PTB-XL cohort}. \rev{Cost runs the same way
-as robustness. The hand-crafted extraction is fifteen times cheaper per record than
-ECGFounder, holds about a third of the resident memory and loads no learned parameters at all
-(Table~\ref{tab:cost}), so the representation that holds up under both stressors is also the
-cheaper one to run.} The
-lead constraint is concrete. The \rev{two} foundation models we test are tied to the 12-lead strip
-format and cannot embed the 2-lead MIT-BIH beats at all, whereas the hand-crafted features
-transfer across lead sets. The raw and reconstruction-autoencoder pipelines, including the
-original project's design, are not viable for label-free arrhythmia structure\rev{ on either
-task}: they are indistinguishable from predicting the majority class \rev{under all five
-clusterers}.
-
-The result also reframes the original master's project honestly. Its conclusion that the
-bottleneck is the representation rather than the clustering algorithm is correct, and we
-have quantified it. Its headline metrics were the artefacts of two avoidable choices,
-purity without a baseline and silhouette on a collapsed embedding. Both are corrected
-here, and both recur often enough in applied clustering to be worth stating plainly.
-
-We claim no methodological novelty. The separability-versus-clusterability phenomenon is
-known \cite{sslclustering2025}, the federated procedure is standard \cite{fedavg2017},
-and the representations are existing tools. \rev{Six things separate this study from the
-closest ECG foundation-model clustering benchmark \cite{lookingbeyond2026}. A hand-crafted
-axis, which turns a comparison between foundation models into a comparison between
-representation families. A supervised ceiling on every representation, linear and
-non-linear, which tells a space that does not hold the label apart from one that holds it in
-the wrong geometry. An out-of-fold test of that distinction, in which a discriminant fitted
-on other patients raises HuBERT-ECG from 0.160 to 0.549 in held-out ARI. A second dataset and
-a second task type, the MIT-BIH beat morphology that benchmark omits. Two cautions a
-practitioner can act on, the degenerate silhouette and the scaler dependence. And two
-deployment stressors with the cost of each representation beside them, which is where the
-balanced-data ranking stops being the useful answer. None of these is a new method, and the
-ordering of the representations is not itself the result.}
-
-\rev{The foundation-model axis is bounded to two models by selection rather than by survey.
-HuBERT-ECG and ECGFounder both publish weights that load into a frozen feature extractor
-without fine-tuning, which is what a benchmark of frozen embeddings requires, and they differ
-in pretraining objective and architecture, a self-supervised transformer
-\cite{hubertecg2024} against a convolutional model pretrained on more than ten million
-recordings \cite{ecgfounder2024}. They also sit at opposite ends of the clustering range
-measured here, 0.885 against 0.195, so the foundation arm is not a single point. What the
-bound does to the conclusions is to scope them: every statement here about a foundation model
-is a statement about one of these two, the deployment comparison is between the hand-crafted
-features and ECGFounder specifically, and none of it extends to ECG foundation models as a
-class. A further model that clusters as well as ECGFounder on balanced data would leave the
-results reported here unchanged, and would have to be measured again under imbalance and
-under federation before the deployment reading could be extended to it.}
+\rev{For the PTB-XL rhythm task and the MIT-BIH morphology task, the results support a
+decision rule.} If a strong public foundation model can be run on balanced data in the
+target 12-lead format, ECGFounder gives the best unsupervised separation here. If the data
+are imbalanced or siloed across sites, if interpretability or low dimensionality matters,
+or if the leads on hand are not the standard twelve, the 16 hand-crafted features are the
+better choice\rev{. On the PTB-XL cohort they were within reach of ECGFounder on balanced
+data (0.711 against 0.885). They led it at the 9:1 ratio (0.265 against 0.063), the one
+imbalanced setting evaluated. Under non-IID federation their retention was 0.92 against
+0.69 for ECGFounder (Table~\ref{tab:fed}). The cost comparison of Table~\ref{tab:cost}
+points the same way: the representation that held up better under both stressors is also
+the lower-cost one to compute. The lead condition rests on the MIT-BIH task: the two
+foundation models we tested could not be applied to its 2-lead beats, whereas the
+hand-crafted feature logic carried to them as the 21-dimensional beat-morphology set.}
+
+\rev{On PTB-XL raw flattening and the conv-AE clustered at chance level under all five
+clusterers. On MIT-BIH, evaluated with three clusterers, the conv-AE reached at most 0.127
+and the raw beat window at most 0.049, against 0.783 for the beat-morphology features. On
+both tasks the raw input and the conv-AE recovered far less of the class structure than
+the hand-crafted features on PTB-XL and the beat-morphology features on MIT-BIH. The
+decision rule above therefore chooses only between ECGFounder and the hand-crafted
+features. Raw flattening reproduces the input of the original project, and its PTB-XL
+result gives that project's conclusion, that the difficulty lies in processing the raw
+signal, in chance-corrected terms.}
+
+\rev{The closest ECG foundation-model clustering benchmark \cite{lookingbeyond2026}
+compared foundation models with one another, and its reading of HuBERT-ECG and ECGFounder
+agrees with the ordering found here. The comparison with hand-crafted features changes
+what that ordering means for a user: the foundation model that led on balanced data did
+not lead at the 9:1 ratio, and its lead narrowed under non-IID federation. The supervised
+ceilings qualify a low score: HuBERT-ECG held the label (ceiling 0.899) without
+clustering by it (ARI 0.195), so a low ARI alone does not show that an embedding lacks
+the clinical information. The MIT-BIH task, on a dataset that benchmark does not include,
+showed the same advantage of features built from the class definitions over raw input and
+the conv-AE for beat morphology.}
+
+\rev{The foundation-model family is bounded to two models by selection rather than by
+survey. HuBERT-ECG and ECGFounder both publish weights that load into a frozen feature
+extractor without fine-tuning, which is what a benchmark of frozen embeddings requires.
+They also differ in pretraining objective and architecture: HuBERT-ECG is a
+self-supervised transformer \cite{hubertecg2024}, and ECGFounder a convolutional model
+pretrained on more than ten million recordings \cite{ecgfounder2024}. Their best ARIs
+here, 0.195 for HuBERT-ECG and 0.885 for ECGFounder, show that the two do not form a
+single point. The bound scopes the conclusions: every statement here about a foundation
+model is a statement about one of these two, the deployment comparison is between the
+hand-crafted features and ECGFounder specifically, and none of it extends to ECG
+foundation models as a class. A further model that clusters as well as ECGFounder on
+balanced data would leave the results reported here unchanged. It would have to be
+measured again under imbalance and under federation before the deployment reading could
+be extended to it.}
 
 \section{Limitations}\label{sec:limitations}
 
-ECGFounder expects 500\,Hz input, and because bulk streaming of the 500\,Hz records was
-rate-limited at the source, the main benchmark embeds the 100\,Hz cohort upsampled to the
-expected length. A consistency check on a 300-record subset, rather than a full re-run,
-shows this does not materially change the result: ECGFounder clusters almost identically at native 500\,Hz and at
-upsampled 100\,Hz (ARI 0.896 versus 0.909, a difference of 0.013), and since upsampling
